--- log ---
2026-05-29T15:30Z Claude created — UX audit finding F10 (verified); "use a prebuilt one" with no source

--- body ---
## Context

The UX audit (0152, PR #174) found that the README's "Install into a
project" step 2 says:

> Drop the `erg` binary into it — use a prebuilt one, or `make build`
> from `src/go/`

with no indication of *where* a "prebuilt one" comes from. A beginner who
doesn't already have the binary has no path forward. Verified: README
line ~98; there is **no** GitHub releases workflow and **no** download
mechanism in `.github/workflows/`. The "prebuilt one" is in fact the
committed bootstrap binary `tickets/erg` (Linux x86-64) that already
travels in every clone — the README simply never says so.

So this is a documentation-clarity fix, not a request to build a release
pipeline (that would fight the "smallest correct system" bar and overlap
with 0151's reproducible-build work).

## Relevant files

- `README.md` — "Install into a project" (~line 95) and "Binary policy"
  (~line 136) sections
- `tickets/erg` — the committed bootstrap binary that IS the prebuilt one

## Actions

1. Reword step 2 to point at the concrete source: the committed
   `tickets/erg` bootstrap binary (Linux x86-64), present in every clone;
   otherwise `make build`. Cross-link the existing "Binary policy"
   section which already explains the committed-binary rationale.
2. Keep the existing "No prebuilt binary for your platform? You don't
   need one" note (README ~line 106) — it already handles non-Linux; just
   make the Linux-x86-64 happy path explicit and self-contained.
3. Do NOT add a releases page or download URL — out of scope; the
   committed binary is the distribution model.

## Test

Source-inspection guard in `tests/test_docs.sh`:

```sh
# The install step must name a concrete binary source, not a bare "prebuilt one"
if grep -A2 'Drop the .*erg.* binary' README.md | grep -qi 'tickets/erg\|make build'; then
    pass "README install step names a concrete binary source"
else
    fail "README install step says 'prebuilt one' with no concrete source"
fi
```

Catches the wrong implementation (vague wording restored), not a mere
rephrasing — the negative control is today's text.

## Verification

- [ ] README install step names the committed `tickets/erg` as the
      prebuilt binary source
- [ ] A reader with only a fresh clone can follow the install path
      without external downloads
- [ ] No releases/download infrastructure added
- [ ] Guard test fails pre-fix, passes post-fix

## Invariants

- Honor the distribution model: committed binary + source, no network
  (offline invariant; no releases pipeline).
- `make test` / `make validate` stay green.

## Exit criteria

- The README install path is self-contained: it tells a reader exactly
  where the prebuilt binary is (the committed `tickets/erg`), guarded by
  a doc test, with no new distribution infrastructure.
